nded in New Haven, Connecticut, in 1882, serving as its birthplace."}, {"sourceSlug": "father-michael-j-mcgivney", "sourceName": "Father Michael J. McGivney", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "knights-of-columbus", "targetName": "Knights of Columbus", "context": "Father Michael J. McGivney founded the Knights of Columbus in 1882 to support Catholic families."}], "places": [{"name": "New Haven, United States", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "Washington, United States", "role": "Major Council"}, {"name": "Toronto, Canada", "role": "Expansion Site"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Knights of Columbus", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "knights-of-columbus"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1046799", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Knights_of_Columbus"], "tags": ["United States", "nonprofit organization", "Americas", "Humanitarian & Relief Organizations", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Knights of Columbus contributed to the historical record of the Modern period. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped institutional landscape and community development. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The Knights of Columbus significantly shaped the Catholic community in North America through its charitable activities and social advocacy, influencing both church and civil society. Over its history, the organization has raised billions for charity, impacting countless lives and reinforcing the Catholic identity in American culture.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-28T20:56:32Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#30398235055", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Catholic fraternal service organization Headquartered in New Haven. Founded 1882.", "newValue": "The Knights of Columbus, founded in 1882 in New Haven, Connecticut, is a prominent Catholic fraternal service organization that has played a significant role in promoting charity, unity, fraternity, and patriotism among its members. Established by Father Michael J. McGivney and a group of parishioners, the organization was created in response to the socio-economic challenges faced by Catholic immigrants in the United States, particularly during a time of widespread anti-Catholic sentiment. Its formation marked a transformative moment in Catholic social action, aligning religious values with community service and mutual support among members, which was crucial in a rapidly industrializing society.

The Knights of Columbus developed a variety of programs and services aimed at supporting members and their families, including life insurance, disability benefits, and scholarships. By the end of the 19th century, the organization had expanded rapidly, boasting over 100 councils across the U.S. and Canada. The Knights also played a pivotal role during the World Wars, encouraging enlistment and providing support to soldiers and their families, which solidified their reputation as a patriotic organization committed to national service.

